    <h1>COPPA Privacy Policy for Kids' Apps: What the Rule and the App Stores Require</h1>
    <p class="last-updated">Published October 17, 2026 &middot; Checked against the amended 16 CFR Part 312</p>

    <p>A COPPA privacy policy is the <strong>online notice</strong> required by <strong>16 CFR 312.4(d)</strong> of the FTC's Children's Online Privacy Protection Rule. If your app or website is directed to children under 13, or you know you collect personal information from a child under 13, the notice must name every operator, describe what you collect from children and how you use and disclose it, publish your written data retention policy, explain any persistent identifiers collected for internal operations, and tell parents how to review, delete and stop further collection. The amended Rule took effect on June 23, 2025, with a compliance date of April 22, 2026.</p>

    <p>For apps, the Rule is only half the gate: Google Play's Families Policy and Apple's Kids Category rules add their own SDK, identifier and advertising restrictions. This guide states what each source requires and cites the section. It is not legal advice.</p>

    <nav class="toc">
      <p class="toc-title">Contents</p>
      <ol>
        <li><a href="#when-coppa-applies">When COPPA Applies to Your App or Website</a></li>
        <li><a href="#what-to-disclose">What a COPPA Privacy Policy Must Disclose, Clause by Clause</a></li>
        <li><a href="#platform-gates">Platform Gates: Google Play Families Policy and Apple's Kids Category</a></li>
        <li><a href="#disclosure-block">Copy-Ready Children's Privacy Disclosure Block</a></li>
        <li><a href="#faq">Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
